### What is JDBC batching and why does it matter?

Without batching, Hibernate sends each INSERT as its own JDBC execute, so inserting 10,000 rows means 10,000 network round trips. With `hibernate.jdbc.batch_size = 50`, Hibernate accumulates up to 50 statements and sends them with JDBC's batch API, cutting round trips by up to 50x.

```yaml
# application.yml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        jdbc.batch_size: 50
```

```text
Without batching (10,000 inserts):
  10,000 round trips x 1ms each = ~10 seconds

With batch_size=50:
  200 batches x 1ms each = ~0.2 seconds (50x faster)
```

### Why is order_inserts required for batching to work?

Batching only works when consecutive statements use the same prepared statement. If your action queue interleaves inserts into different tables, JDBC cannot batch them. `order_inserts` reorders the queue so all inserts for one table are grouped.

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        order_inserts: true
        order_updates: true
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Holding;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class InterleavedInsertDemo {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public InterleavedInsertDemo(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void createPortfolioWithHoldings() {
        // Without order_inserts, queue is:
        // INSERT account, INSERT holding, INSERT account, INSERT holding
        // JDBC cannot batch because the prepared statement keeps changing

        // With order_inserts, queue becomes:
        // INSERT account, INSERT account, INSERT holding, INSERT holding
        // Each group batches together!

        for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) {
            em.persist(new Account("SAV" + i, "Customer " + i, BigDecimal.ZERO));
            em.persist(new Holding("SYM" + i, 100, new BigDecimal("1000.00")));
        }
        em.flush();
    }
}
```

```text
Without order_inserts:
  Account INSERT, Holding INSERT, Account INSERT, Holding INSERT...
  Each switch breaks the batch = 100 individual round trips

With order_inserts:
  50 Account INSERTs batched, then 50 Holding INSERTs batched
  = 2 round trips
```

### Why does IDENTITY generation kill batching?

With `GenerationType.IDENTITY`, the primary key is generated by the database via auto-increment. Hibernate must execute each INSERT immediately to read the generated key back. Since inserts cannot be deferred, they cannot be batched.

```java
// IDENTITY: batching silently disabled
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;
// persist(entity) -> INSERT runs NOW -> id read back
// Cannot defer -> cannot batch

// SEQUENCE: batching works
@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "g")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "g", sequenceName = "account_seq", allocationSize = 50)
private Long id;
// persist(entity) -> id pre-fetched from sequence -> INSERT deferred
// 50 inserts batched into one round trip
```

```text
IDENTITY (50 inserts):
  persist -> INSERT -> read id -> persist -> INSERT -> read id ...
  = 50 round trips, batch_size setting IGNORED

SEQUENCE (50 inserts, allocationSize=50):
  1 sequence call -> 50 ids pre-fetched
  50 persists -> INSERTs deferred
  flush -> 50 inserts in 1 JDBC batch
  = 2 round trips total
```

### How do all the settings work together?

The complete configuration for efficient batch inserts combines SEQUENCE generation, batch_size, order_inserts, and the flush+clear pattern.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "acct_gen")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "acct_gen", sequenceName = "account_seq",
                       allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
    private String holderName;
    private BigDecimal balance;
}
```

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        jdbc.batch_size: 50
        order_inserts: true
        order_updates: true
        generate_statistics: true
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/bankdb?reWriteBatchedInserts=true
```

```java
package com.bank.batch;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class EfficientBatchInsert {

    private static final int BATCH = 50;
    private final EntityManager em;

    public EfficientBatchInsert(EntityManager em) {
        this.em = em;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void importAccounts(int count) {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            em.persist(new Account("Customer " + i, BigDecimal.ZERO));
            if ((i + 1) % BATCH == 0) {
                em.flush();
                em.clear();
            }
        }
        em.flush();
    }
}
```

```text
Complete pipeline for 10,000 inserts:
1. SEQUENCE: 200 calls (50 ids each) = 200 round trips for ids
2. flush every 50: 200 JDBC batches of 50 inserts = 200 round trips
3. Total: ~400 round trips instead of 10,000
4. With reWriteBatchedInserts: each batch becomes a multi-row INSERT
5. Memory: bounded at 50 entities (flush+clear)
```

### How do you verify batching is working?

Enable Hibernate statistics to see actual batch counts. If the statistics show N individual executions instead of N/batch_size batches, something is wrong.

```yaml
spring:
  jpa:
    properties:
      hibernate:
        generate_statistics: true
```

```text
Expected statistics output (10,000 inserts, batch_size=50):
  HHH000117: 200 JDBC batches executed
  statements executed: 200 (not 10,000)

If you see:
  statements executed: 10,000
  -> Batching is NOT working!
  -> Check: IDENTITY ids? Missing order_inserts? batch_size not set?
```

### What about PostgreSQL reWriteBatchedInserts?

On PostgreSQL, adding `reWriteBatchedInserts=true` to the JDBC URL tells the driver to rewrite a JDBC batch of individual INSERT statements into a single multi-row INSERT, which is significantly faster.

```text
JDBC URL:
jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/bankdb?reWriteBatchedInserts=true

Without rewrite (batch of 50):
  INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1, 'A', 0);
  INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (2, 'B', 0);
  ... 48 more ...
  (50 statements in one batch)

With rewrite (batch of 50):
  INSERT INTO accounts VALUES (1,'A',0),(2,'B',0),...,(50,'Z',0);
  (one multi-row INSERT -- even faster)
```

### What are common mistakes with batch insert configuration?

The most common mistake is configuring batch_size but using IDENTITY generation, which silently disables batching.

```java
// WRONG: batch_size set but IDENTITY kills it
// hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50 -- IGNORED!
@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
private Long id;

// WRONG: batch_size set but order_inserts missing
// Interleaved tables break batching even with batch_size

// WRONG: allocationSize=1 on sequence
@SequenceGenerator(name="g", sequenceName="seq", allocationSize=1)
// 10,000 inserts = 10,000 sequence calls!

// CORRECT: all settings aligned
@SequenceGenerator(name="g", sequenceName="seq", allocationSize=50)
// hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50
// hibernate.order_inserts=true
```

```text
Common mistakes:
1. IDENTITY + batch_size -- batching silently disabled
2. Missing order_inserts -- interleaved tables break batches
3. allocationSize=1 -- one sequence call per insert
4. Not verifying with generate_statistics
5. @DynamicUpdate + batching -- varying SQL breaks batch grouping
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer verifies batch formation with statistics, matches allocationSize to batch_size, uses reWriteBatchedInserts on PostgreSQL, and knows that `@DynamicUpdate` conflicts with batching because it produces varying SQL per entity.

```text
Senior checklist:
1. SEQUENCE generation with allocationSize matching batch_size
2. hibernate.jdbc.batch_size, order_inserts, order_updates all set
3. PostgreSQL: reWriteBatchedInserts=true
4. Verify with generate_statistics=true
5. @DynamicUpdate conflicts with batching (varying SQL)
6. Combine with flush+clear for memory management
7. Consider per-batch commits for very large loads
8. @Version optimistic locking still works with batching
```
